A("Python evaluates default arguments once when the function is defined. Both independent jobs therefore append to the same default list. I would call add twice without passing rows and assert that each job should start empty; the existing implementation fails that isolation test. Use rows=None and allocate a fresh list inside the function. If the caller intentionally passes a list, document whether the function mutates it or returns a copy. This is shared mutable state, not a threading problem; concurrency can make it worse but is unnecessary to reproduce it.","Using rows=[] again inside another default parameter leaves the same bug.","How would you preserve explicit caller-owned accumulation without sharing implicit state?",["Definition-time evaluation","Independent-invocation test","Fresh default state","Document mutation"],"def add(row, rows=None):\n    if rows is None:\n        rows = []\n    rows.append(row)\n    return rows\n\nassert add('a') == ['a']\nassert add('b') == ['b']\nshared = []\nassert add('c', shared) is shared"),
A("Use a generator with a caller-owned Stats object. Increment seen for each physical line, parse JSON, verify a mapping with nonempty string tenant_id and repo_id, count rejected lines and yield a typed record for valid lines. Keep malformed input distinct from programmer errors: catch JSONDecodeError, not every exception. A generator runs lazily, so counters are partial until exhausted. The caller holds the Stats reference and inspects it during or after iteration. File lifetime belongs to a with-open block surrounding consumption. Memory is O(one parsed line plus caller state); an unbounded single line still needs a record-size limit.","Returning stats from a generator is not a convenient ordinary return value for a for loop.","How do you impose line-size limits and report partially consumed input?",["Lazy execution","Explicit stats ownership","Narrow exceptions","Input-size bound"],"from dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable, Iterator\nimport json\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Record:\n    tenant_id: str\n    repo_id: str\n\n@dataclass\nclass Stats:\n    seen: int = 0\n    valid: int = 0\n    invalid: int = 0\n\ndef records(lines: Iterable[str], stats: Stats) -> Iterator[Record]:\n    for line in lines:\n        stats.seen += 1\n        try:\n            value = json.loads(line)\n        except json.JSONDecodeError:\n            stats.invalid += 1\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, dict) or any(\n            not isinstance(value.get(k), str) or not value[k].strip()\n            for k in ('tenant_id', 'repo_id')\n        ):\n            stats.invalid += 1\n            continue\n        stats.valid += 1\n        yield Record(value['tenant_id'].strip(), value['repo_id'].strip())\n\ns = Stats()\nassert list(records(['{bad}', '{\"tenant_id\":\"t\",\"repo_id\":\"r\"}'], s)) == [Record('t', 'r')]\nassert (s.seen, s.valid, s.invalid) == (2, 1, 1)"),
A("For X shaped (1000,32) and w shaped (32,), X*w broadcasts w across rows and returns (1000,32): every feature contribution remains separate. X@w or (X*w).sum(axis=1) returns (1000,), one dot-product score per row. sum(axis=0) instead aggregates across observations and returns 32 feature totals. Check shape, finite values and dtype first. If an intercept exists, add it after the row score. Broadcasting is useful but can create unexpectedly huge temporaries when singleton dimensions are placed incorrectly.","X*w is not already a one-score-per-row prediction.","What happens when w has shape (32,1) or X contains NaN?",["Broadcasting shape","Correct reduction axis","Dot-product equivalence"],"# Requires locally installed NumPy. No network needed at execution.\nimport numpy as np\nX = np.arange(12, dtype=float).reshape(3, 4)\nw = np.array([1., 2., 3., 4.])\nweighted = X * w\nscores = X @ w\nassert weighted.shape == (3, 4)\nassert scores.shape == (3,)\nassert np.allclose(scores, weighted.sum(axis=1))"),
A("Take 10,000 repositories. There are 100 truly high-risk cases, of which 90 are detected. Among 9,900 safe cases, 5% produces 495 false alerts. Total alerts are 585; the fraction truly high risk is 90/585≈15.38%. Equivalently, P(risk|alert)=0.90×0.01/(0.90×0.01+0.05×0.99). Sensitivity is P(alert|risk), not P(risk|alert). I would check whether deployment prevalence matches the evaluation population before estimating reviewer load. A low base rate makes false-positive control operationally important.","Do not interpret 90% sensitivity as 90% precision.","If prevalence increases to 10% with identical conditional rates, recompute precision and reviewer demand.",["Correct prior","True/false alert counts","Conditional-probability direction"]),
A("A 95% frequentist confidence-interval procedure covers the fixed population parameter in 95% of repeated experiments under its assumptions. It does not assign a 95% posterior probability to this particular interval. p=0.03 means data at least this extreme have probability 0.03 under the specified null model; it is not the probability the null is true. I would report effect size and uncertainty, sampling design, independence, and number of comparisons. Statistical significance alone does not prove useful business impact, generalization or causality. A randomized design and valid measurement support stronger causal claims than correlation alone.","p<0.05 is not proof of meaningful improvement or a 97% probability of success.","How would correlated tasks or multiple prompt comparisons alter your inference?",["Coverage interpretation","Null-conditional p-value","Effect size","Assumptions and multiplicity"]),
A("Repeatedly picking the best score on the same 40 tasks turns that set into training feedback. The winner may exploit task-specific noise, and many comparisons increase false discoveries. Use a development set for iteration and a protected held-out set for the final comparison. Run configurations on identical tasks, inspect paired differences, repeat stochastic generations, and report task/slice counts and uncertainty. Predeclare the primary metric and safety gates; use appropriate multiple-comparison controls when making inferential claims. Bootstrap independent tasks or clusters at the correct unit, not dependent tokens. A tiny average gain is insufficient when critical safety slices regress.","Calling the reused set a test set does not make it independent.","What if five questions are near-duplicates from one repository?",["Adaptive overfitting","Protected holdout","Paired evaluation","Dependence and practical significance"]),
