A("For one batch, put the model in training mode, clear prior gradients, compute logits, compute the appropriate loss, call backward to populate parameter gradients, then optimizer.step to update weights. Without zero_grad, gradients accumulate across batches. That is a bug in ordinary per-batch updates, but can be intentional for larger effective batches if loss scaling and step frequency are designed correctly. Inputs, labels, logits and parameters have different roles and expected shapes/dtypes. Validation uses eval mode and disables gradient recording, without weight updates. Monitor loss and gradient norms rather than assuming every decreasing training curve generalizes.","model.eval() does not disable autograd; no_grad does not set eval mode.","How would you implement four-microbatch gradient accumulation without changing the intended gradient scale?",["Forward/loss/backward/update","Gradient accumulation","Train versus validation","Shape/loss contract"]),
A("Freeze a representative input and compare exact transformed inputs before comparing weights. Check train/eval mode for dropout and batch normalization, preprocessing and tokenizer versions, class-index mappings, dtype/device, precision settings, batch shape, randomness and the loaded architecture. Use eval plus no_grad/inference_mode for inference. Compare intermediate outputs to find the first divergence, and record tolerances for numerical variation. A state_dict can be identical while surrounding inference behavior differs. Retraining before diagnosing the first mismatch hides a reproducibility defect and may introduce another one.","Matching weight files is not matching the complete inference pipeline.","How could changing batch size expose a batch-normalization or padding defect?",["Input parity","Mode parity","Version/schema parity","Intermediate comparison"]),
A("In next-token training, position i must not attend to future token positions j>i, because those tokens would be unavailable at generation time. A causal mask suppresses their attention scores before softmax. Bidirectional encoders generally see both preceding and following context for representation objectives; autoregressive decoders see the prefix to predict the next token. Encoder-decoder models use separate source encoding and conditioned decoding. Attention and padding masks have different jobs. Validate normalization and verify future-token attention probability is zero. The choice follows the training objective and task, not merely the model name.","A causal mask is not an authorization filter or a way to remove positional information.","Where does cross-attention differ from decoder self-attention?",["Future-token leakage","Encoder/decoder objective","Mask application","Normalization test"]),
