A("Inspect the exact query, authorized corpus version, ranked IDs and labeled relevant passages. Relevant-looking is not sufficient: check that the necessary facts and versions are present. Inspect the final assembled context for truncation, duplicated chunks, lost warnings and ordering. Then run generation with a known-good gold context; if the answer still fails, isolate synthesis, instruction conflicts, citation verification or model limits. Measure retrieval recall/ranking separately from claim correctness, groundedness, completeness and abstention. Log versioned traces with access controls and redaction. This staged intervention locates the failure boundary more precisely than swapping models blindly.","Good document titles can hide missing spans or incompatible versions.","If the answer is fully supported by the wrong outdated document, which metrics detect the problem?",["Gold relevance","Actual final context","Controlled intervention","Independent quality metrics"]),
A("Enforce tenant and document ACLs in trusted services before candidate retrieval and again before source delivery/context assembly. Identity and permission version belong in cache keys; sharing a semantic query key across tenants is unsafe. Propagate deletes/revocations to indexes and caches within a documented bound; authorize in-flight reads again at delivery or cancel them according to policy. Version documents/embeddings/index generations and never mix incompatible vector spaces. Use partitioning/sharding and admission control based on measured ingestion/query load. Revocation guarantees and eventual index lag must be explicit. A service account able to read all documents does not grant each end user that access.","Metadata filters supplied by the model are not an authorization boundary.","How do you prove a revoked document cannot leave a cache during an active request?",["Trusted ACL enforcement","Cache identity/version","Revocation semantics","Index lifecycle"]),
A("Use deterministic transitions for scope, authorization, build/test gates, budget enforcement and approval, because they need reproducible policy. Use model judgment for ambiguous source interpretation, evidence-grounded recommendations and proposed custom transformations. Treat its output as a proposal with schema, evidence and bounded tools. Start with one auditable workflow before adding agent swarms: more agents add coordination cost and can share the same misconception. Persist plan, revision, allowed paths and receipts so runs can resume safely. Measure completion accuracy and unsafe actions on adversarial fixtures, not just conversation quality.","Planning agents should not decide whether failed gates can be ignored.","Which of your current stages require judgment, and which must be fixed policy?",["Policy versus judgment","Bounded proposals","Durable evidence","Agent evaluation"]),
A("A timeout is an uncertain observation; the remote deployment may already exist. Move the run to a pending/reconcile state and query the remote operation by stable idempotency key or request ID. If the API guarantees idempotency, retry using the same key under a bounded policy. Otherwise inspect actual side effects before deciding; do not invent exactly-once behavior. Persist the approved artifact hash, target, request and receipts. Escalate unresolved or conflicting state rather than duplicating deployment. A new key on retry defeats idempotency. Approval must still match the exact artifact and destination.","Timeout does not mean no side effect; an exit code is not a complete deployment receipt.","What if reconciliation succeeds but the artifact hash differs from the approved hash?",["Uncertain outcome","Stable key","External reconciliation","Artifact-bound approval"]),
