"Use a frozen dataclass for normalized data, explicit validation for incoming strings and a separate result/error type or exception policy. Strip identifiers, validate allowed language and numeric ranges, parse aware timestamps, and reject NaN/Infinity. Types help tools; they do not enforce data correctness at runtime.",
"A generator avoids loading all input, but a caller that list()s its output, a dedup dictionary growing with unique keys, or exact median aggregation still retains O(n) state. Bound line size and aggregation, or spill/sort in external storage. State the whole-pipeline bound, not just the parser bound.",
"A many-to-many join creates one output row per matching pair. Inspect key uniqueness and row counts before/after merging, use validate='one_to_one' or the intended relationship, include tenant in composite keys and define missing-key behavior. Do not remove duplicates blindly after multiplying business records.",
"Publish output atomically with schema/input/run IDs, reconciled valid/invalid/superseded counts and an explicit partial-success policy. Downstream must know which records were excluded. Make retry identity stable and preserve original input plus quarantined error categories without logging secrets.",
"For exact dedup keep latest (tenant,repo) state or use an external key store/sort. For exact median keep/sort values or external-sort bounded partitions; a fixed-memory approximate quantile must be labeled approximate. With 2 million keys, measure per-key overhead before promising a 256 MB bound."
